---README.md---
1: # Web Template
2: 
3: This template serves one web application on two HTTP listeners. The frontend is built with Vite and React, and the backend is an Express server that serves both `/api/*` routes and the compiled frontend from `frontend/dist` on each listener
4: 
5: ## Project Layout
6: 
7: - `frontend/`: React, Vite, Tailwind, Vitest, and frontend tests.
8: - `backend/`: Express, SQLite runtime helpers, backend Vitest tests, and Playwright E2E tests.
9: - `backend/src/app.js`: Express app, `/api/health`, static frontend hosting, and SPA fallback.
10: - `backend/src/index.js`: Backend process entrypoint.
11: - `backend/src/page-data.js`: Current-route data adapter; reuse authorized API reads.
12: - `backend/src/page-bootstrap.js` and `frontend/page-data-plugin.js`: Real initial
13:   data in built/development HTML, with request identity and safe JSON serialization.
14: - `frontend/src/state/route-data.ts`: Shared in-memory snapshots, write publication,
15:   stale-read invalidation and explicit session reset; usage in state/README.md.
16: 
17: ## Prerequisites
18: 
19: - Node.js and npm.
20: - A shell environment that can install npm dependencies in both `frontend` and `backend`.
21: 
22: ## Install Dependencies
23: 
24: Install each package independently:
25: 
26: ```bash
27: cd backend
28: npm install
29: 
30: cd ../frontend
31: npm install
32: ```
33: 
34: ## Build and Run
35: 
36: The production-style runtime is backend-led. Build the frontend first, then start the backend:
37: 
38: ```bash
39: cd frontend
40: npm run build
41: 
42: cd ../backend
43: npm run start
44: ```
45: 
46: The backend listens on `PORT` (default 3000) and 3301, because platform readiness and Web acceptance tests can use different ports. `SPEC_BASE_PORTS` overrides the complete comma-separated list; duplicate ports are used only once. Each list
47: 
48: Health check:
49: 
50: ```bash
51: curl http://127.0.0.1:<port>/api/health
52: ```
53: 
54: ## Development Commands
55: 
56: Backend development server:
57: 
58: ```bash
59: cd backend
60: npm run dev
61: ```
62: 
63: Frontend development server:
64: 
65: ```bash
66: cd frontend
67: npm run dev
68: ```
69: 
70: The Vite dev server proxies `/api` to the configured backend port. It also reads
71: initial page data from that backend before serving HTML; start both servers.
72: `ARC_WEB_PORT` selects the backend for Vite (default 3000). Production still uses
73: the built frontend served by Express, not standalone `vite preview`.
74: 
75: ## Tests
76: 
77: During agent development, use the managed entry from the project root:
78: 
79: ```bash
80: python3 scripts/check.py frontend backend e2e
81: python3 scripts/check.py backend --file tests/a-specific-feature.test.js
82: ```
83: 
84: The simple workflow installs package dependencies and a matching Chromium before
85: coding. The entry builds the frontend for E2E, starts its own non-TTY backend with
86: two temporary ports and an isolated project-local database, checks readiness and
87: stops only its own process group, including on failure or cancellation. It reports
88: real positive test counts; skipped, flaky, empty or missing reports cannot pass.
89: If package/lock files change, run `npm ci` in that package before checking again.
90: 
91: Successful development evidence is reused only for the same source, dependencies,
92: environment and test selection, with intact evidence and build artifacts.
93: The controller-owned `.haiknow/progress.md` journal alone does not invalidate
94: success; other knowledge files, Markdown and database fixtures still do.
95: Use
96: `--fresh` to investigate a concrete new concern; a fresh failure invalidates the
97: older success. Evidence is in `.arc/local-check`; `cached: true` means reuse, not a
98: new execution. Final controller acceptance always uses a separate cold copy and
99: does not trust this cache. Per-command and startup timeouts can be set with
100: `--timeout` and `--startup-timeout`; they do not change the agent's total budget.
101: 
102: The lower-level commands below remain available for diagnosis. Direct Playwright
103: commands require an existing server; the managed entry owns that setup normally.
104: 
105: Frontend unit/integration tests:
106: 
107: ```bash
108: cd frontend
109: npm run test
110: ```
111: 
112: Backend unit/integration tests:
113: 
114: ```bash
115: cd backend
116: npm run test
117: ```
118: 
119: Backend E2E tests use Playwright and live under `backend/test-e2e`:
120: 
121: ```bash
122: cd backend
123: npm run test:e2e
124: ```
125: 
126: Run backend Vitest tests followed by E2E tests:
127: 
128: ```bash
129: cd backend
130: npm run test:all
131: ```
132: 
133: ## Database Notes
134: 
135: - Runtime database helpers live under `backend/src/database`.
136: - The default database file is `database.db`, unless `ARC_DB_FILE` or `DATABASE_FILE` is set.
137: - Test helpers create isolated SQLite files under `.arc-test-db`.
138: - `npm run db:seed` runs the template seed entrypoint.
139: - `npm run db:prepare:e2e` prepares an isolated E2E database.
140: 
141: Use `db_runtime.js` for request-time `run/get/all/exec` and `withTransaction`.
142: The shared connection queues complete transactions together with ordinary
143: operations; a separate request cannot read uncommitted data or have its write
144: rolled back by another request. Await database calls inside the transaction
145: callback. Its `tx` methods and public runtime helpers join the same transaction;
146: use the current transaction instead of nesting `withTransaction`. A retained `tx`
147: handle rejects calls after the callback completes. Failures roll back and release
148: the queue for subsequent requests.
149: 
150: Raw `getDb()` access is for initialization/lifecycle code, not request handlers.
151: Raw connections, hand-written BEGIN/COMMIT and database resets bypass the runtime
152: queue; close/reset/switch the database only after outstanding work has completed.
153: Keep transactions short and do not wait for unrelated network work inside one.
154: 
155: ## Async resource integration
156: 
157: The reusable [UI contracts](frontend/src/ui/README.md) cover native labeled fields,
158: forms, record/dialog scope, stable page regions and explicit async write outcomes.
159: The blank application contains no example business records or routes. Compose the
160: components with names and data from the current requirements.
161: 
162: `frontend/src/state` contains `useAsyncResource` and its `useSession` bootstrap
163: consumer, also exported by `frontend/src/ui`. See [the state contract](frontend/src/state/README.md)
164: for adapters, applied/error/superseded results and server-side ordering limits.
165: The blank App does not mandate authentication; connect the shared session consumer
166: only when the current application needs it.
167: 
168: ## Requirement source queries
169: 
170: The controller creates frozen `.haiknow/modules/index.json` and module views.
171: Query only the facts needed for the current module instead of dumping the complete
172: acceptance inventory:
173: 
174: ```bash
175: python3 scripts/contract_context.py query --module CURRENT_REQ --kind requirements
176: python3 scripts/contract_context.py query --module CURRENT_REQ --kind initial
177: ```
178: 
179: `requirements`/`all` queries retain ancestor and dependency context. Complete input
180: files remain authoritative. The default simple workflow does not create or consume
181: manual UI/initial-state mappings: implement the original requirements and test them.
182: The optional E2E-first workflow enables the separate `runtime` query and `bind`
183: command in its frozen module view; those commands reject use when disabled.
184: A binding is never an executed browser check or an E2E pass.
